    "title": "Hypothetical Inference Speeds (18 & 24 t/s): What Metrics Are We Missing?",
    "body": "We're analyzing a hypothetical local inference scenario involving two configurations that produced 18 and 24 tokens per second (t/s) on identical hardware with a single prompt. While these speeds are a starting point, they don't tell the whole story. This hypothetical experiment explicitly did not measure critical factors like time to first token (TTFT) or the quality of the output. A model might be fast in terms of t/s but feel slow if it takes a long time to start generating text, or if the generated text is inaccurate and requires re-prompting. Furthermore, as this was a test with one prompt on one machine, it cannot establish a universal performance winner. Different prompts, system loads, and model architectures could yield different results. These hypothetical numbers serve to illustrate that raw t/s is just one piece of the puzzle.",
